"""
css_decoder_dechirp.py  —  CSS decoder: dechirp + rotation PLL + single-loop timing.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Why dechirping instead of FFT cross-correlation
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
v2 cross-correlated the raw received signal against a real-valued reference
chirp in the FFT domain.  With >50 % of each chirp obscured by broadband
noise, the noise contribution to the XC peak dominates the signal term,
collapsing the detection margin.

Dechirping collapses the problem to a single-bin detection:

    r(t) = A·chirp_up(t−τ) + n(t)
    bb(t) = r(t) · conj(chirp_up_analytic(t))
          = A·exp(−j·2π·k·τ·t)·exp(j·const)  +  white noise

A matched chirp produces a pure tone at FFT bin  b = −k·τ·nfft/fs.
An unmatched (opposite) chirp produces only a broadband noise floor.
A narrow search window (±search_bins around bin 0) rejects all out-of-band
noise, giving ~38 dB more margin than matched filtering for sps=6615.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Three root causes of CRC failure — all diagnosed from the test log
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

ROOT CAUSE 1 (fatal) — post_lock_update only called during data, not preamble
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PLL locks at sym=18.  Sync is not acquired until sym=26.  For 8 symbols
(Phase 2a, preamble search), timing error accumulates with zero correction.
Any residual from the coarse rotation lock bakes into best_offset and
compounds with any ongoing clock offset.

FIX: post_lock_update is called on EVERY symbol after PLL lock, before the
preamble/data branch.  The TED continuously tracks timing from the first
post-lock symbol.

ROOT CAUSE 2 (fatal) — kp=0.04 takes ~113 symbols to converge
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The timing loop equation is  τ[n] = τ[0]·(1−kp)^n.
To reach 2 % of the initial error takes  log(0.02)/log(1−kp)  symbols:
    kp=0.04  → 90 symbols  (way beyond the 51.9 s frame count)
    kp=0.50  →  6 symbols  (well within the 13-symbol preamble)

The log shows ted drifting −2 → −10 over successive frames: the loop
correction is smaller than the ongoing clock offset, so the error grows.

FIX: default kp raised to 0.50.  max_hop_adj (±sps//8 ≈ ±826 samples)
prevents overshoot.  Loop is stable for kp ∈ (0, 1).

ROOT CAUSE 3 (destabilising) — dual dc_centre + best_offset tracking
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The previous version maintained a separate dc_centre tracker for the FFT
search window AND a best_offset tracker for the time-domain window.  These
are coupled: when post_lock_update shifts best_offset, the actual DC-bin
position of the dechirped tone changes.  But dc_centre lags behind
(alpha=0.10 → τ_lag ≈ 10 symbols).  For those 10 symbols the scorer
searches the OLD neighbourhood, sees noise instead of the signal peak, and
feeds a corrupted TED back into the correction that just moved the window.
The two integrators fight each other.

FIX: dc_centre is eliminated entirely.  The scorer always searches ±sb bins
around bin 0.  The ONLY clock correction is best_offset (time domain).
This is a single-integrator loop — stable, transparent, and correct.

═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
Timing PLL — physical derivation (single-loop)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
For a received symbol delayed by τ_samp samples relative to the window start:

    dechirped tone frequency  f = −k·τ_samp/sps   [normalised, k=BW/dur]

    FFT bin (signed)          b = −BW·τ_samp·nfft / (sps·fs)

    Inverse (_b2s constant)   τ_samp = −b · sps·fs / (BW·nfft)

TED sign convention:
    Symbol LATE  (τ_samp > 0):  b < 0  →  timing_err = b·_b2s < 0
                                adj = −kp·timing_err > 0  →  best_offset ↑  ✓
    Symbol EARLY (τ_samp < 0):  b > 0  →  timing_err > 0
                                adj < 0  →  best_offset ↓  ✓

Loop gain analysis:
    After one symbol:  τ_new = τ_old·(1−kp)
    kp = 0.50  →  τ halves every symbol; < 2 % of τ₀ after 6 symbols.
    kp must be < 1.0 for stability.

Search window:
    Coarse rotation lock has step  sps // n_rotations.
    Worst-case residual  ≈  step / 2  samples  =  sps / (2·n_rotations).
    Corresponding bin     ≈  BW·(sps/(2·n_rotations))·nfft / (sps·fs)
                          =  BW·nfft / (2·n_rotations·fs)
    search_bins is auto-computed to cover this plus a 12-bin margin.

Frame format: [seq:1 B][data:chunk_size B][crc32:4 B]  (unchanged)

Usage:
    python css_decoder_dechirp.py                    # uses css_decode_config.json
    python css_decoder_dechirp.py my_config.json
    python css_decoder_dechirp.py --print-schema
"""

